\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces How the Voronoi face is built, and what bounds it, on the primary section. \textbf  {a},\nobreakspace  {}Segmented polygons and their centroids around a typical cell (blue). \textbf  {b},\nobreakspace  {}The polygons are set aside; each neighbour contributes the perpendicular bisector (dashed) of the segment joining the two centroids. \textbf  {c},\nobreakspace  {}The bisectors cut one another, and what survives between the cell and a neighbour is the face $f_{ij}$ (blue, length in $\mu $m); the shaded area is the cell's Voronoi region. Panels a--c share one scale. \textbf  {d},\nobreakspace  {}A dense neighbourhood and \textbf  {e},\nobreakspace  {}a cell at a tissue border, at one scale, each with the $30\,\mu \text  {m}$ clip disc (dotted). At the border the unclipped region (dashed) runs into the gap, the disc bounds it, and the dark stretch of the outline between two faces is the clip arc. \textbf  {f},\nobreakspace  {}Face length against centroid distance for every edge of the section. Clipping bounds each face by the chord $2\sqrt  {R^2-(d_{ij}/2)^2}$ of the disc, with $R = 30\,\mu \text  {m}$, and removes every edge with $d_{ij} \ge 2R$; the model keeps edges up to $40\,\mu \text  {m}$ (unshaded).\relax }}{20}{figure.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:voronoi-face}{{3}{20}{How the Voronoi face is built, and what bounds it, on the primary section. \textbf {a},~Segmented polygons and their centroids around a typical cell (blue). \textbf {b},~The polygons are set aside; each neighbour contributes the perpendicular bisector (dashed) of the segment joining the two centroids. \textbf {c},~The bisectors cut one another, and what survives between the cell and a neighbour is the face $f_{ij}$ (blue, length in $\mu $m); the shaded area is the cell's Voronoi region. Panels a--c share one scale. \textbf {d},~A dense neighbourhood and \textbf {e},~a cell at a tissue border, at one scale, each with the $30\um $ clip disc (dotted). At the border the unclipped region (dashed) runs into the gap, the disc bounds it, and the dark stretch of the outline between two faces is the clip arc. \textbf {f},~Face length against centroid distance for every edge of the section. Clipping bounds each face by the chord $2\sqrt {R^2-(d_{ij}/2)^2}$ of the disc, with $R = 30\um $, and removes every edge with $d_{ij} \ge 2R$; the model keeps edges up to $40\um $ (unshaded).\relax }{figure.caption.32}{}}
\newlabel{fig:voronoi-face@cref}{{[figure][3][2147483647]3}{[1][19][]20}}
